      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous text, we explored an axiom stating that any value added to 0 equals itself (Additive Identity). Additionally, we learned that advancing a number in the numerical sequence is equivalent to adding 1 to the previous number",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6e3666ca-88e4-4b5e-a717-c42056476a77",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking Among the Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: On axioms</b><br /><br />If an axiom exists for adding zero, could there also be axioms for adding other numbers?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this text, we will delve deeper into addition and explore how we can move more efficiently along the numerical axis.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_axiom",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Addition Order\n\nThe addition operation has a fundamental property, which is also an axiom. While we have not yet fully explored it, this property will be essential in guiding our studies. Let’s revisit the representation of a numerical axis:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"chart\"><div class=\"tick\"><span>0</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>1</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>2</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>3</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>4</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>5</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>6</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>7</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>8</span></div><div class=\"tick\"><span>9</span></div><div class=\"tick arrow rightarrow\"></div></div></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of an axis with numbers. The numbers are placed below the line, and an arrow on the right end indicates the direction in which the values increase. The source code used to generate the CSS-based image is available on <htcite0>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c7edc93c-0a41-4753-8f89-9e52904ff003",
                     "text": "W3 Schools",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Based on what we have learned previously, let’s begin by working with the number 5. On the axis shown above, we can interpret a person standing on the number 5 as being five units to the right of the origin. This means the person has taken five steps from 0. Additionally, we know that adding 0 to any number leaves the number unchanged. For example",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6e3666ca-88e4-4b5e-a717-c42056476a77",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Walking Among the Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ":"
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><i><b><b>Calculation 1</b>:</b> Adding 0 and 5.</i></p></div></div></center><p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: On Inversion</b><br /><br />Would the result be the same if, instead of calculating <b>5 + 0</b>, we calculated <b>0 + 5</b>?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From the axiom we learned earlier, we know that adding zero to any number results in the same number. Therefore, both **0 + 5** and **5 + 0** yield the same result, as we are simply adding 0 to the number 5 in both cases.\n\nThis principle applies to other numbers as well. For example, if we add 4 and 1, and then reverse the order by adding 1 and 4, the result remains the same:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><p class=\"desc\"><i><b><b>Calculation 2</b>:</b> Addition of integer numbers.</i></p></p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We can visualize these calculations using the numerical axis in **Figure 1**. In the first case, we start at position 4 and advance 1 step to reach position 5. In the second case, we start at position 1 and advance 4 steps, again reaching position 5.\n\nBased on these observations, we can define the **Commutative Axiom** (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7a9c733b-ca13-40f5-8849-19012540b028",
                     "text": "Ernst, Dana.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"><li><i><b>Commutative Axiom:</b> For any numbers <b>a</b> and <b>b</b>, the order of addition does not change the result:<br /> a + b = b + a</i></li></ul></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To conclude this section, let’s apply the Commutative Axiom to Calculation 2:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgGrape\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgGrape', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Calculation 2 represented with fruits.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this image, we observe the sum of one fruit with four other fruits resulting in five fruits independent of the order.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we discovered the <b>Commutative Axiom of Addition</b>: when we add two numbers, the order does not matter—the result is always the same. Whether we calculate 4 + 1 or 1 + 4, we arrive at 5.\n\nWe also saw this principle in action using the numerical axis: starting at 4 and moving 1 step forward lands us at the same position as starting at 1 and moving 4 steps forward. The path changes, but the destination does not.\n\nThis axiom is one of the building blocks of mathematics. In the next text, we will use it—along with what we already know about adding zero—to explore how to add larger numbers efficiently.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "If I calculate <b>5 + 4</b>, would the result be different from <b>4 + 5</b>?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "As mentioned in the <b>Commutative Axiom of Addition</b>, the order in which we add numbers does not change the final result."
      },
      {
         "question": "A student claims that <b>3 + 7</b> is different from <b>7 + 3</b> because the numbers are in a different order. Is the student correct?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "The student is not correct. According to the <b>Commutative Axiom of Addition</b>, changing the order of the numbers does not change the result. Both 3 + 7 and 7 + 3 equal 10. You can verify this on the numerical axis: starting at 3 and advancing 7 steps reaches the same position as starting at 7 and advancing 3 steps."
      }
   ],
